{
  "id": "COMPOUND:34435 - PROTEIN:3694",
  "verbalization": "Ent-Gloeosteretriol appears to exert its biological effects through a series of mechanistic pathways that ultimately target the Deoxyuridine 5'-triphosphate nucleotidohydrolase, putative. The evidence suggests that Ent-Gloeosteretriol acts as a target for multiple enzymes, including mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 11 and solute carrier organic anion transporter family member 2A1, which in turn are targets of methohexital and benzocaine, respectively. These compounds all ultimately inhibit the Deoxyuridine 5'-triphosphate nucleotidohydrolase, putative, indicating a shared mechanism of action involving the inhibition of nucleotide metabolism. The presence of multiple independent targets and their common downstream effect on the same enzyme suggests a potential role for Ent-Gloeosteretriol in modulating nucleotide homeostasis, possibly through the inhibition of this critical enzyme. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of the Deoxyuridine 5'-triphosphate nucleotidohydrolase, putative, as a key mediator in the biological effects of Ent-Gloeosteretriol, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction and the precise mechanism of action.",
